N={}
N[(1,0)]=[
("What it is","Research suggests that 30 to 60% of secondary students are disengaged from school, and motivation, emotions and engagement are critical links to learning. This chapter is an overview of classroom practices teachers can build into instruction to support students' motivation, emotions and engagement, and through them learning and achievement."),
("The good news","Intelligence explains much of school success but is hard to change. Motivation and engagement, by contrast, respond to the classroom and to interventions. Students arrive with motivational resources: they want to explore interests, live out values, pursue goals, grow competent and connect with others. <i>In plain words:</i> the fuel is already there; teaching can light it or smother it."),
("The plan","Five sets of motivation, each with its supporting practices: (a) <b>perceived competence</b>, (b) <b>autonomy</b>, (c) <b>interest and value</b>, (d) <b>growth mindsets, adaptive attributions and mastery goals</b>, (e) <b>relatedness and belonging</b>. The authors chose five categories deliberately and included practices with convincing or promising evidence; the list could be longer, for example practices for emotions."),
("How to read it","Each section has the same build: the theories, then a table of practices (what the teacher does; how it works and what to watch out for; which other kinds of motivation it also supports), then the evidence. Throughout, the authors note which students and contexts change how well a practice works, with special attention to marginalized or minoritized students."),
]
N[(2,0)]=[
("How to read it","You met these five in readings 1 and 2 under theory names: perceived competence (self-efficacy, expectancies), autonomy (self-determination theory), value and interest, growth mindset and mastery goals (attributions, mindsets, goals), and belonging (relatedness). The chapter does what reading 1 asked for: it combines the theories, through the classroom."),
("What comes next","One step per support, each with its table, then how to use them together. Keep in mind: the tables say a practice \"primarily\" supports one kind of motivation; the last column lists the others it also supports."),
]
N[(3,0)]=[
("The idea","A climbing wall with holds that are just within reach builds confidence; a wall too easy or too hard does not. Several theories stress <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"compbel\">competence beliefs</b>: self-efficacy, expectancies, academic self-concept, the need for competence, goal setting. They differ subtly, but all say students must believe they can control their own results."),
("The evidence","Positive competence beliefs shape goals, effort, persistence, emotions, self-regulation and, in the end, achievement. Self-efficacy predicts engagement, attendance, willingness to set hard goals, intentions to persist in college and course grades. Limited research suggests the links may vary somewhat by grade, gender, culture and race."),
("Structure, challenge, feedback","Clear, consistent expectations, rules and routines support all four sources of self-efficacy. Tasks should slightly exceed current skills, with <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"scaffold\">scaffolding</b> and step-by-step difficulty. Feedback should be informational: what went well, how the strategy helped, what to improve. Critical feedback is safer when it targets the skill, not the person, comes warmly and with tips; <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"wisefb\">wise feedback</b> adds high expectations and confidence that the student can meet them."),
("Models and strategies","Peer models, and models who show struggle as part of learning, help students find strategies to try. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfreg\">Self-regulation instruction</b> teaches cognitive, metacognitive and emotional strategies; self-regulation and competence beliefs strengthen each other. Praise should target strategy and effort, not traits. <b>Examples:</b> motivational scaffolds in online maths modules predicted higher self-efficacy (Valencia-Vallejo et al., 2018); reflection assignments on goals, strategies and self-evaluation raised writing students' motivation and achievement (MacArthur &amp; Philippakos, 2013)."),
("So what","A teacher builds \"I can do this\" by arranging real, visible progress on work hard enough to count. Several cautions concern minoritized students: low challenge, critical feedback without high expectations, and missing role models weigh most where low expectations already exist."),
]
N[(4,0)]=[
("The idea","Autonomy support is an interaction style: the teacher tries to see the lesson through the student's eyes, with curiosity, openness and empathy, so that students feel they learn willingly, because it fits their own values, interests and goals."),
("The evidence","Correlational and experimental research shows that students of more autonomy-supportive teachers have more autonomous motivation, engagement, self-regulation, self-esteem and well-being, and higher achievement, from preschool to graduate school and across abilities and cultures, perhaps especially in adolescence."),
("Choice, with three principles","(1) Having options is not the same as feeling autonomous: choices given in a controlling way, steered toward one option, tied to rewards, or about things students do not care about, help less. (2) Choice can backfire when students lack the competence to choose well, or when there are too many or too similar options; then it needs guidelines. (3) Culture and relationships change what choosing means: letting a trusted person choose can itself feel autonomous."),
("Beyond choice","<b>Take perspectives:</b> make room for students' questions, opinions, criticism and negative feelings. <b>Use them:</b> build lessons around interests, let students personalize tasks. <b>Speak informationally:</b> \"could\", \"consider\" instead of \"must\", \"should\". <b>Reduce control</b>, which can breed disengagement and amotivation. <b>Encourage <span class=\"tm\" data-g=\"agentic\">agentic engagement</span>:</b> students help shape the lesson. Rationales also support autonomy (step 5)."),
("So what","Autonomy support is less a set of tricks than a stance: start from where the student is. It links back (choice can backfire for students who lack competence) and forward: a student who chooses freely finds the content more valuable and interesting."),
]
N[(5,0)]=[
("The idea","A lesson on percentages lands differently if students use it to compare phone plans they actually want. Four frameworks explain why value and interest matter: expectancy-value theory (more attainment, intrinsic or utility value raises persistence, performance and choices), self-determination theory (learning works better when students endorse it as their own), interest theories (situational interest responds most to the classroom) and culturally relevant education."),
("The evidence","Situational interest improves engagement, memory, self-regulation, strategy use, persistence, achievement and course choice, and repeatedly sparking it helps individual interest develop. Recall the four kinds of value: <b>attainment</b> (important to who you are), <b>intrinsic</b> (enjoyable), <b>utility</b> (useful elsewhere) and <b>cost</b> (the downsides, such as time or anxiety)."),
("Rationales and purpose","A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"rationale\">rationale</b> explains why something is useful or important; it works best when it builds on what students know, takes their perspective, is specific and concrete, and appeals to prosocial or intrinsic goals. Because value is personal, students can also find it themselves: writing how material relates to their lives, discussing its value, sharing peers' reasons. Which route works depends on initial interest, competence and background."),
("Four more practices","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"ctl\">Contextualized teaching</b> embeds content in meaningful, real-world situations. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"cre\">Culturally relevant education</b> uses students' cultures and experiences as teaching material, bridges home and school, and can empower students to act on problems in their community. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"activel\">Active learning</b> makes students think about what they do. <b>Digital technology</b> can spark interest through novelty, especially combined with collaboration or games and kept low in cognitive load."),
("So what","Value cannot be handed over; it has to connect to what the student already cares about. That is why \"explain why it matters\" works for one student and not another. Next: what helps when work gets hard."),
]
N[(6,0)]=[
("The idea","Two students fail the same test: one thinks \"I need a better strategy\", the other \"I'm not a maths person\". Three related theories say students benefit when the emphasis is on <b>developing</b> competence: attribution theory (how students explain results), mindsets (beliefs about intelligence) and achievement goal theory (developing versus demonstrating competence). The shared message: with effort, persistence and good strategies, people grow."),
("The evidence","Students who believe intelligence can grow persist more after failure, feel more positive and less anxious, and achieve more; those who see it as fixed read failure as proof of low ability and become helpless. But interventions are mixed: mindset programmes have a small average effect (Sisk et al., 2018), and the classic praise findings have not been fully replicated."),
("Training beliefs and explanations","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"growthm\">Mindset training</b> teaches that the brain grows with challenge, with peer quotes and a \"saying-is-believing\" letter to a struggling peer; effects may depend on the school's achievement level, peer norms and what teachers say about effort. <b>Attributional retraining</b> teaches students to see successes as controllable and failures as unstable, often with videos of older students. Teachers can simply point to effort and strategy as causes."),
("Praise and the goal structure","Praise the process (effort, strategy, perseverance), not the person (\"so smart\"). A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"mgs\">mastery goal structure</b> has meaningful, challenging tasks, evaluation based on improvement and standards rather than comparison, autonomy support and a caring climate with high expectations. Grades alone went with performance goals, comments with mastery goals (Koenka et al., 2021); students who saw a class as grade-focused adopted more performance goals (Church et al., 2001)."),
("So what","Telling students \"you can grow\" is the weakest version. The strong version is a classroom where everything, from praise to grading, says the same thing; a mastery goal structure is built from the practices of all the tables."),
]
N[(7,0)]=[
("The idea","Students try to live up to the values of people they feel connected to. Attachment theory, self-determination theory (relatedness) and the belongingness hypothesis all stress belonging: students who feel close to a teacher internalize that teacher's values about learning."),
("The evidence","From primary school to college, relatedness to teachers and peers goes with intrinsic motivation, grades, self-efficacy and engagement, and perceived teacher support goes with belonging. Belonging is often more strongly linked to retention, persistence and grade gains for minoritized students than for white students."),
("Why the focus on minoritized students","Belonging matters for everyone, but through interpersonal and systemic racism, minoritized students more often lack it in academic settings. The idea that they do not value school has been consistently dispelled. So the chapter focuses on practices for Black, Latinx, Native and other students of colour: community, meaningful relationships, and seeing themselves accepted and represented, also in the curriculum."),
("The practices","<b>Teacher-student connections</b>, from formal mentoring (Larose et al., 2005) to everyday respect, with psychological safety as the key (Anderman, 2003). <b>Collaboration</b> (group projects, think-pair-share, peer teaching) with common goals, small groups, positive interdependence and individual accountability: an \"interpersonal opportunity structure\" (Gray et al., 2018). <b>Belonging messages</b>: you are valued, and feeling out of place is common and temporary. <b>Culturally relevant education</b> that honours students' identities."),
("So what","Belonging closes the five, and in our reading loops back to the first: a student who feels safe and accepted is more likely to try hard things. Last step: using the five together."),
]
N[(8,0)]=[
("The idea","Three closing themes: <b>overlap</b> (practices and kinds of motivation are not separate boxes), <b>universality versus precision</b> (most practices help broadly, yet need tailoring) and <b>partnership</b> (researchers, educators and policy-makers must work together so motivating teaching becomes routine)."),
("The evidence","Most practices were chosen because their support is relatively well established, including randomized experiments. Some are promising but with more limited causal evidence or unclear classroom use: encouraging agency, digital technology, relatedness practices and culturally relevant education. Overall, the authors judge the support broad enough to use the recommended practices."),
("Overlap","One practice can feed several kinds of motivation, and one kind can be fed by several practices: many routes to the same goal. But one practice is seldom enough, and many work best in combination: structure with autonomy support and a warm climate, collaboration with structure, technology carrying other practices."),
("Precision","The same practice can mean different things to different students. Confident students may relish choice and rationales; less confident students may find them demoralizing. Belonging and growth messages can be vital for students whose belonging and competence are constantly questioned, and feel patronizing to others. Practices work best when matched to the specific challenge a student faces in a specific setting."),
("So what","No checklist, but a way of thinking: find out which support this student is missing, choose practices for it, combine them. That is also the theme's answer to its opening question."),
]

